---
// Real features of this repository, built with its own workflow, ordered by
// their risk level. The high-risk candidates are those of the audit on #235;
// #241 is the medium feature of the run records with a review finding. Every figure comes from a source
// named beside it: the Issue and its published review and triage comments,
// the pull request description that finish-feature.sh and publish-feature.sh
// wrote, or the run record of the feature (.agents/records/feature-<n>.jsonl
// on the maintainer's machine, not in the repository).
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHead from "../components/PageHead.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Table from "../components/Table.astro";
import { href, issue, pull, repoFile } from "../lib/links";

const comment = (number: number, id: string) => `${issue(number)}#issuecomment-${id}`;

// The other high-risk features of the audit's candidate list, from their run records and pull requests.
const features = [
  { issue: 189, pull: 194, anchor: "owner", change: "Compute the risk level of a feature from one policy", rounds: "3", findings: "none", verify: "556, 575, 572", run: "four runs; the implementer resumed by hand" },
  { issue: 193, pull: 199, anchor: "repair", change: "Treat performance budgets as measurements", rounds: "4", findings: "1 major, re-raised once; 1 minor; all fixed", verify: "761, 762, 761", run: "1 h 18 min" },
  { issue: 204, pull: 205, anchor: "short", change: "Show the reviewer no change in paths the fingerprint leaves out", rounds: "1", findings: "none", verify: "909", run: "38 min" },
  { issue: 16, pull: 213, anchor: "short", change: "Add the feature clarification gate", rounds: "1", findings: "none", verify: "849", run: "26 min" },
];

// The principal source files each pull request changed (git show --stat of its merge commit).
const changed: Record<number, string[]> = {
  194: ["scripts/lib/risk.sh", "scripts/check-risk.sh", "scripts/finish-feature.sh", "scripts/publish-feature.sh", "tests/risk-test.sh"],
  199: ["scripts/verify.sh", "scripts/lib/verification.sh", "scripts/run-feature.sh", "tests/verify-test.sh"],
  205: ["scripts/review-feature.sh", ".agents/prompts/reviewer.md", "tests/review-feature-test.sh"],
  213: ["scripts/run-feature.sh", ".agents/prompts/implementer.md", "scripts/lib/completion.sh", "tests/run-feature-test.sh"],
};

// The review rounds of #217: the run record and the comments on the Issue.
const rounds = [
  { round: 1, kind: "complete feature", verdict: "CHANGES REQUIRED", finding: "M1, major: earlier approved decisions can disappear when a newer triage is invalid", decision: "FIX_NOW", link: comment(217, "6482184594") },
  { round: 2, kind: "the fixes", verdict: "no findings", finding: "–", decision: "–" },
  { round: 3, kind: "complete feature", verdict: "CHANGES REQUIRED", finding: "M1, major: predecessor validation allows re-raises to weaken a FIX_NOW gate", decision: "FIX_NOW", link: comment(217, "6482598113") },
  { round: 4, kind: "the fixes", verdict: "no findings", finding: "–", decision: "–" },
  { round: 5, kind: "complete feature", verdict: "PASS WITH MINOR FINDINGS", finding: "MIN1, minor: the review schema makes previous required", decision: "ACCEPT", link: comment(217, "6483051924") },
];

// The review rounds of #241: the run record, the comment on the Issue and the description of PR #249.
const mediumRounds = [
  { round: 1, kind: "complete feature", verdict: "CHANGES REQUIRED", finding: "M1, major: examples do not link each candidate to its source files", decision: "FIX_NOW", link: comment(241, "6531925164") },
  { round: 2, kind: "the fixes", verdict: "no findings", finding: "–", decision: "–" },
  { round: 3, kind: "complete feature", verdict: "PASS", finding: "–", decision: "–" },
];
---
<Base title="Examples" description="Features of this repository built with Agent Codeyard, ordered by risk: a high-risk and a medium-risk run followed from the Issue to the pull request, with agents, review rounds, triage, verification evidence and the merge.">
  <PageHead title="Examples" lede="This site is built with Agent Codeyard. These are real runs of this repository, ordered by risk, followed from the Issue to the merged pull request with the records each step left.">
    <ol class="contents">
      <li><a href="#levels">The examples by risk</a></li>
      <li><a href="#e2e">High risk, end to end: #217</a></li>
      <li><a href="#features">High risk, more runs: #189, #193, #204, #16</a></li>
      <li><a href="#medium">Medium risk: #241</a></li>
      <li><a href="#absent">What is not shown</a></li>
      <li><a href="#sources">Where the figures come from</a></li>
    </ol>
  </PageHead>

  <Section id="levels" title="The examples by risk">
    <Fragment slot="note">The levels as each run recorded them. <a href={href("features/#risk")}>Risk-proportionate workflow</a>.</Fragment>
    <p class="lede">The risk level of a feature decides which model writes it and what the review rounds require. This repository runs the shipped risk policy, without a <code>profile</code> line, so every feature is medium or high.</p>
    <Table>
      <caption>From the risk lines of each pull request description and the run records. Run: from the start of <code>run-feature.sh</code> to the pull request.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">level</th><th scope="col">example</th><th scope="col">why this level</th><th scope="col" class="num">rounds</th><th scope="col">run</th><th scope="col">merge approval</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><span class="lvl high">high</span></th><td data-col="example"><a href="#e2e">#217</a>, <a href={pull(222)}>PR #222</a></td><td data-col="why this level">It changes the reviewer's contract, the review schema and gate scripts</td><td data-col="rounds" class="num">5</td><td data-col="run">1 h 30 min</td><td data-col="merge approval">required</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><span class="lvl medium">medium</span></th><td data-col="example"><a href="#medium">#241</a>, <a href={pull(249)}>PR #249</a></td><td data-col="why this level">Website pages that no class of the policy names: the default</td><td data-col="rounds" class="num">3</td><td data-col="run">13 min</td><td data-col="merge approval">not required</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><span class="lvl">low</span></th><td data-col="example" class="off">–</td><td data-col="why this level">Exists only under <code>profile: light</code>, which this repository does not use</td><td data-col="rounds" class="num off">–</td><td data-col="run" class="off">–</td><td data-col="merge approval" class="off">–</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </Table>
    <p>Under the shipped policy, medium and high run the same rounds: full verification, full review and full triage. What the level changed between these two runs is the model of the fixes and the owner's approval of the merge.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="e2e" title="High risk, end to end: #217">
    <Fragment slot="note"><a href={issue(217)}>Issue #217</a>, <a href={pull(222)}>PR #222</a>. One unattended run of <code>run-feature.sh</code>, 28 October 2026.</Fragment>
    <p class="lede">The feature that gave every later review round the decisions of the earlier ones. It came out of Codeyard's analysis of its own runs, and it is followed here from its requirements to the merge.</p>

    <h3>Requirements</h3>
    <p>The run analysis of 28 October 2026 found that in five runs the reviewer raised a finding again as unresolved after it had been fixed, and that in one feature it raised findings again that the triage had accepted. The Issue asks that every review after round 1 receives the decisions of all earlier rounds, that a finding names the one it continues (<code>unresolved</code> or <code>worsened</code>), and that the stored review is checked against the recorded decisions. It has six acceptance criteria; the last one reads “No gate is weakened: a FIX_NOW finding that is really unresolved is still reported and still blocks.” Out of scope: the triage rules, the round limit, and the owner-exception mechanism.</p>

    <h3>Risk level and agents</h3>
    <dl class="facts">
      <dt>risk</dt><dd><span class="lvl high">high</span>: the feature changes <code>.agents/prompts/reviewer.md</code> and <code>.agents/schemas/feature-review.schema.json</code>, existing contract files; <code>scripts/lib/review-data.sh</code> and <code>scripts/review-feature.sh</code>, listed as high in <code>risk.conf</code>; and four existing test files. The reviewer also classified the impact on the architecture as major.</dd>
      <dt>implementer</dt><dd><code>claude opus</code>. The first session started before there was a diff, so the level was still the default, medium. That gap became <a href={issue(227)}>#227</a>: you can now raise the level before the first session (<a href={pull(229)}>PR #229</a>).</dd>
      <dt>fixes</dt><dd><code>claude fable</code>, selected by the line <code>triage-implementer@high</code></dd>
      <dt>review</dt><dd><code>codex gpt-6-luna</code>, read-only</dd>
      <dt>triage</dt><dd><code>claude sonnet</code>, read-only</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3>Implementation and tests</h3>
    <p>The change adds the earlier decisions to the reviewer's context in <code>scripts/review-feature.sh</code>, the optional <code>previous</code> field to the finding schema, and the validation in <code>review_artifact_errors</code> of <a href={repoFile("scripts/lib/review-data.sh")}><code>scripts/lib/review-data.sh</code></a>. A new test file, <a href={repoFile("tests/review-data-test.sh")}><code>tests/review-data-test.sh</code></a>, covers each invalid case; existing tests only gained blocks, no assertion was changed. Each re-raise becomes a <code>finding</code> event in the run record, and <code>report-runs.sh</code> counts them per feature.</p>

    <h3>Independent review and triage</h3>
    <Table>
      <caption>Rounds 1, 3 and 5 reviewed the complete feature, 2 and 4 only the fixes. The triage of each round with findings is published on the Issue, marked as approved by an unattended run.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col" class="num">round</th><th scope="col">reviewed</th><th scope="col">verdict</th><th scope="col">finding</th><th scope="col">decision</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rounds.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" class="num">{row.link ? <a href={row.link}>{row.round}</a> : row.round}</th>
            <td data-col="reviewed">{row.kind}</td>
            <td data-col="verdict" class="k">{row.verdict}</td>
            <td data-col="finding" class:list={{ off: row.finding === "–" }}>{row.finding}</td>
            <td data-col="decision" class:list={["k", { off: row.decision === "–" }]}>{row.decision}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </Table>
    <p>Round 3 is the review doing its job: the fix of round 1 was correct, but the new rule could still let a re-raised finding lower its severity, which would have weakened the very gate the Issue protects. Round 5 found one minor point, and the triage accepted it with a rationale, quoted below.</p>
    <pre role="region" class="excerpt" aria-label="Excerpt of the triage of round 5"><span class="src">{issue(217).replace(/^https:\/\//, "")} · round 5 · triage, excerpt</span>### MIN1. The review schema makes `previous` required

- Severity: minor
- Rationale: Listing `previous` in `required` while allowing null
  is deliberate. It follows the strict structured-output
  convention, where every property is required and optionality
  is expressed as nullable. [...] The runtime validators accept
  artifacts that omit it, so old artifacts stay valid [...]
  The mismatch is cosmetic and blocks nothing.</pre>

    <h3>Verification evidence</h3>
    <p>The full verification ran three times, once after each writing session: 1046, 1049 and 1118 seconds. Eight more times a step required a verification and reused the recorded pass, because the content had not changed since. Every review report names the reviewed tree and the verification that passed for it.</p>

